      "content": "While real estate developers and commercial lenders may not overtly assess neighborhoods based on racial demographics, in practice, there can be subconscious biases and systemic factors that influence their decisions. Areas with predominantly Black or Hispanic populations are often subject to lower property value appraisals and less favorable financing terms due to historical data and market biases. Furthermore, redlining practices and inequitable lending terms that were historically targeted toward minority communities can still influence these perceptions. Meanwhile, predominantly White neighborhoods are often perceived as 'safer' investments with more reliable returns, hence may attract more investment. However, this is not ethically justified and reflects deep-seated issues within the industry that have far-reaching consequences. The impact of the social justice movement, along with shifts toward more equitable practices, has begun to influence some changes, though the influence of these historical biases remains evident in the market. Addressing these injustices requires active intervention, both at the regulatory level and within the industry, to ensure that investment patterns and public perceptions shift toward a more equitable focus.",
